# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Secrets and account material (§11 S15). Nothing in this block may ever be
# committed, to any branch, including "temporarily".
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.maFile
# Token write-back keeps `<file>.maFile.bak` and writes `<file>.maFile.tmp`, and
# both hold the same secrets as the original. `*.maFile` does NOT match either —
# the pattern requires the name to END with .maFile — so they need naming here.
*.maFile.*
*.bak
*.tmp
maFiles/
manifest.json
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
vault.json
vault.json.*
*.session

# Build output.
# NOTE: /build/ is deliberately NOT ignored — §10.2 puts icons, the
# electron-builder config and entitlements there, and they must be committed.
dist/
out/
release/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Editors / OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json

# Logs — these are the most common accidental secret-leak vector, so no
# exceptions here either (§11 S4).
*.log
npm-debug.log*
logs/
coverage/

# Test fixtures are the ONE exception: they are synthetic and deliberately
# tracked so the parser corpus survives. They contain no real account material.
!spike/fixtures/*.maFile

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The support server's runtime state. The schema is created by the server at
# startup; the database fills with reporters' contact details and report text,
# and the attachments directory with their screenshots. None of it is ever
# ours to commit. (An early schema-only tickets.db was tracked once — verified
# row-free before it was untracked.)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
tickets/*.db
tickets/*.db-wal
tickets/*.db-shm
tickets/*.db-journal
tickets/attachments/

# Scratch: generated PDFs and other working files. Never part of a release.
tmp/

# SBOM documents and the self-test fixtures the release workflow writes beside
# them. Generated per run and never reviewed as source; committing one would
# make a stale inventory look like an authoritative one.
*.spdx.json

# The tree those documents describe, and the scanner configuration that decides
# what is catalogued in it, both written by
# .github/scripts/assemble-shipping-tree.mjs. Anyone can now run that script
# locally — `sbom.yml` runs it on every pull request and reproducing a failure
# by hand is the point — so its output has to be ignorable rather than a
# surprise in `git status`.
sbom-root/
sbom-syft.yaml
